1. Introduction

Scope

This document defines coding standards for C++, C#, and Java in [[Project Name]]. These standards aim to ensure code quality, maintainability, and consistency across the project.

Audience

Developers, testers, reviewers, and stakeholders involved in the development lifecycle of [[Project Name]].

2. General Coding Standards

2.1 Code Formatting
	•	Rule 2.1.1: Use 4 spaces for indentation; avoid tabs.
	•	Rule 2.1.2: Limit line length to 120 characters.
	•	Rule 2.1.3: Use consistent brace styles (K&R or Allman).

2.2 Naming Conventions
	•	Rule 2.2.1: Use camelCase for variables and methods (employeeName).
	•	Rule 2.2.2: Use PascalCase for classes (UserProfile).
	•	Rule 2.2.3: Use UPPER_SNAKE_CASE for constants (MAX_RETRIES).

2.3 Commenting Guidelines
	•	Rule 2.3.1: Document public methods with parameters, return types, and functionality.
	•	Rule 2.3.2: Avoid commented-out code unless tagged with TODO or FIXME.

2.4 File Structure and Organization
	•	Rule 2.4.1: Organize files logically in folders (Models, Services, etc.).
	•	Rule 2.4.2: Separate interface definitions from their implementations.

2.5 Code Documentation
	•	Rule 2.5.1: Use tools like Doxygen (C++), XML comments (C#), or Javadoc (Java).

2.6 Security Best Practices
	•	Rule 2.6.1: Validate all user inputs.
	•	Rule 2.6.2: Avoid hardcoding sensitive information.

3. Language-Specific Standards

3.1 C++ Standards
	•	Rule 3.1.1: Use smart pointers for memory management.
	•	Rule 3.1.2: Avoid raw pointers unless absolutely necessary.

3.2 C# Standards
	•	Rule 3.2.1: Use dependency injection for managing dependencies.
	•	Rule 3.2.2: Avoid exposing fields; use properties.

3.3 Java Standards
	•	Rule 3.3.1: Use @Override annotations for overridden methods.
	•	Rule 3.3.2: Avoid static imports for readability.

4. Error Handling and Logging
	•	Rule 4.1: Log meaningful error messages, avoiding sensitive data.
	•	Rule 4.2: Use structured error handling (e.g., exceptions).

5. Testing and Quality Assurance
	•	Rule 5.1: Maintain a minimum of 80% unit test coverage.
	•	Rule 5.2: Use mocking frameworks for dependency testing.

